menu "CSI motion detector configuration"

    config CSI_MOTION_SSID
        string "Access point SSID"
        default "myssid"

    config CSI_MOTION_PASSWORD
        string "Access point password"
        default "mypassword"

    config CSI_MOTION_INTERVAL_MS
        int "Milliseconds between pings"
        range 5 1000
        default 20
        help
            Also sets the CSI rate, and therefore what the detector can see. A
            person walking takes a few hundred milliseconds to cross a link, so
            20 ms (50 records per second) gives plenty of samples per event.

    config CSI_MOTION_CALIBRATION_RECORDS
        int "Records to average into the baseline before watching"
        range 20 2000
        default 250
        help
            At the default 20 ms interval, 250 records is about five seconds.
            Stay out of the path between the board and the AP for that long
            after boot, or you calibrate your own presence into the baseline and
            the detector then reports motion when you leave.

    config CSI_MOTION_THRESHOLD_PERCENT
        int "Motion threshold, percent mean deviation"
        range 1 200
        default 15
        help
            The score is the mean relative change in per-sub-carrier amplitude
            against the baseline, so 15 means "on average 15 percent away from
            what the room looked like when still".

            This number is not portable. It depends on the distance between the
            board and the AP, on what is in the room, and on the band. Watch the
            MOTION lines with the room empty and with someone walking through,
            then set it between the two.

    config CSI_MOTION_HOLD_MS
        int "Minimum milliseconds to hold the motion state"
        range 0 10000
        default 1500
        help
            Stops one person crossing the link from being reported as a rapid
            series of separate events.

    config CSI_MOTION_FORCE_LLTF
        bool "Report L-LTF instead of HT/VHT/HE-LTF"
        default n
        help
            Leave this at n unless you have a specific reason.

            The obvious argument for y is stable record shape: traffic from a
            real AP arrives in whatever PHY its rate control picked, so record
            length keeps moving between 490, 234, 114 and 106 bytes, and the
            detector can only compare sub-carrier k against baseline
            sub-carrier k when k means the same frequency every time.

            The argument against is stronger. On the C5, forcing L-LTF also
            switches the buffer to 12-bit packing - four bytes per tone instead
            of two - which is undocumented and is the single most common way to
            get silently wrong CSI on this chip. The shared component decodes
            both correctly, so it is safe either way here, but Espressif's own
            recommendation for human sensing is to leave it off:

              "234 subcarriers are sufficient to capture human body state
               features [...] 8-bit precision is completely adequate for
               detecting sitting/standing/walking"

            The detector does not need a globally fixed shape anyway - records
            whose shape does not match the baseline are counted and discarded
            rather than compared, and the summary reports them as "wrong-shape".
            If that count is high, the cheaper fix is to pin the link (an
            11n 40 MHz connection gives a steady 234) rather than to force
            L-LTF.

    config CSI_MOTION_VAL_SCALE
        int "CSI value scaling (val_scale_cfg)"
        range 0 8
        default 0
        help
            Scales the reported I/Q values; ESP-IDF documents only the range,
            0-8 on the C5. Because the detector works on relative change against
            its own baseline, a change here mostly rescales the score, so if you
            do change it, re-tune the threshold too.

endmenu
